  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe'll use adaptive dimension parametrization, where the sequence length and structure are defined by amenable peak ratios (APRs). The approach calculates the theoretical minimum objective value based on the length and structure of a sequence family. Key components include:\n   - Explicit length calculation based on peak ratios\n   - Cross-validation of introduced constraints with objective reduction\n   - Projection-based updates that escape suboptimal solutions\nThe rationale is that this allows targeting the exact objective function value (2*n*M/(s\u00b2)) without chasing surrogate performance, ensuring top-tier optimization while managing runtime efficiently.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe algorithm provides: \n   - Target length (n_out): squared by parameterized number of distinct bands\n   - Convolution constraints (M_lower_bound): computed from underlying peak distribution\n   - Objective: exact fraction of our function, not an approximation\nSequence construction: geometric progression under fixed norm (sum=1) ensures non-negative data and direct objective trace.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nCore implementation:\n   - Data loading: Use the parent sequence if available, else generate new.\n   - Primary search: Compute ideal length via theoretical minima and validate with objective.\n   - Secondary updates: Projections and localized perturbations adjust the sequence to maintain constraints.\n   - Resource handling: Parallelize evaluations and enforce strict time budgeting during search.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variants\nKey choices:\n   - Start with a normalized geometric sequence, then vary band lengths.\n   - For high-dimensional solutions (n > 300), use multi-shifted convolution constraints.\n   - Fallback: The simplex projection ensures sequences adhere to spendable objective space when dimensions are too high.\n\n5. Time Budget and Robustness\n   - Dominant factors: Objective evaluations and constraint projections for n_ar_d.\n   - Allocate min 30 seconds per sequence evaluation, max 5% spare.\n   - Fallback if duration exceeds 100s: use simplex-normalized restart.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Retest final candidate against the original `evaluate_sequence`.\n   - Print only objective values < 1.5027 accurate to 8 decimal places.\n   - Return empty list if complete failure, but ensure best offer is given.",
